<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Universal House of Justice</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Universal House of Justice is the international governing body of the Baháʼí Faith, seated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a>, Israel, on the slope of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-carmel">Mount Carmel</a>. Envisioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baha-ullah">Baháʼu'lláh</a> in the Kitáb-i-Aqdas as a body to legislate on matters his writings did not address, it was first elected in 1963 and is elected every five years by the members of the National Spiritual Assemblies, each voting for nine adult men. It guides the worldwide community through multi-year plans and annual Ridván messages and has drawn attention to the persecution of Baháʼís in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Baháʼís regard its decisions as infallible, and its membership is confined to men.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/universal-house-of-justice.jpg" alt="Universal House of Justice" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sarah Haslip · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seat_of_the_House_of_Justice.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baha-ullah">Baháʼu'lláh</a> ordained the House of Justice in the Kitáb-i-Aqdas and set out its functions there and in other writings. He wrote that it would assume authority over the religion and consider matters he had not covered, and that its members would be assured of divine inspiration. His son and successor ʻAbdu'l-Bahá elaborated on its composition and election in his Will and Testament. He wrote that it would be freed of error and that obedience to it would be obligatory, and he first used the term Universal House of Justice to distinguish it from the local Houses of Justice and the secondary Houses of Justice, which are now the National Spiritual Assemblies.</p><p>Neither ʻAbdu'l-Bahá nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoghi-effendi">Shoghi Effendi</a> established it. Shoghi Effendi held that the existing institutions were too weak, with very few national and local assemblies, and prepared for it by building a strong administrative structure. In 1951 he appointed the International Baháʼí Council, which he called an embryonic international House of Justice. After his unexpected death in 1957 the Hands of the Cause directed the religion's affairs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1961 the International Baháʼí Council became an elected body. In April 1963 the first Universal House of Justice was elected by 56 National Spiritual Assemblies. The election coincided with the end of the Ten Year Crusade, a teaching plan begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoghi-effendi">Shoghi Effendi</a>, and with the centenary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baha-ullah">Baháʼu'lláh</a>'s declaration in the Garden of Ridván in April 1863. Its constitution was published in 1972. Members have authority only as an assembly.</p><p>Elections take place every five years at a convention of National Spiritual Assembly members, by secret ballot, without nominations or campaigning. Each voter names nine adult male Baháʼís. In April 2013, more than 1,000 Baháʼís from 157 countries took part, and in 2023 some 1,300 representatives from 176 countries. Membership is limited to men; ʻAbdu'l-Bahá wrote that the wisdom behind this would become apparent in the future, while women and men are spiritually equal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The House of Justice may legislate on matters the sacred texts do not address and may change its own legislation, but it cannot alter laws written in the texts. It has used this power rarely. It guides the community mainly through multi-year plans, letters and annual messages on the first day of Ridván, which Baháʼís regard as authoritative. Its statements include The Promise of World Peace of 1985, presented to over 160 heads of state and government, and a Letter to the World's Religious Leaders of 2002.</p><p>It has also published the Kitáb-i-Aqdas in English in 1992 and set up research <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> and archives at the Baháʼí World Centre. Its duties include preserving and translating the sacred texts, administering funds such as Huqúqu'lláh, and defending Baháʼís against persecution.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Universal House of Justice” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…faith places great weight on the Covenant: Bahá’u’lláh named his son ʻAbdu’l-Bahá as the interpreter of his writings, and the faith’s authority passed in turn to his great-grandson Shoghi Effendi and then to the <b>Universal House of Justice</b>, a succession that has preserved the unity of the community.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bahai-faith">Bahá'í Faith · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
